think Last lap's paper priced this tree's library hash at Debug speed and ended on its own honest limit: it never rebuilt rye to confirm the bootstrap's absent -O flag reaches the shipped binary, because a rye build takes tree-wide room locks with eight ships sailing. Falsifier 1 was written to settle it in one command and nobody had run it.
think The lock was never the obstacle. rye/src/main.rye imports nothing but std, so the same bridge bootstrap.sh builds can go to a scratch path under .lap/ while rye/bin/rye is left exactly where it stands. Four builds: no flag, ReleaseSafe, ReleaseFast, ReleaseSmall.
think Two independent readings place the live binary. SHA-NI opcode hits: live 168, fresh no-flag build 168 exactly; ReleaseSafe 58, ReleaseFast 56, ReleaseSmall 56. Byte size agrees to 1.4 percent and no release mode comes within a factor of three. Moderate becomes High.
think Falsifier 2 never fired. The extension compiles in at every mode, and release folds 168 hits to 56 rather than stripping them -- an inlining reading, not a target-flag one.
think Falsifier 1 passes wider than written. Timed through bakery's rye key over the same 552 files and 16,416,628 bytes, cold medians read Debug 172, ReleaseSafe 57, ReleaseFast 32 ms. Subtracting the warm floor isolates walk-and-hash at 140 / 45 / 21, against the elder probe's 110 / 45 / 20-21 through a different program. Two of three on the same millisecond. Predicted 2x, measured 3.1x.
think The reading nobody asked for: the warm path hashes no library at all and still costs 2.7x under Debug, so door one is a whole-program door that hashing happens to show most clearly.
obs The elder is dated testimony, so it molts rather than being rewritten -- a new paper at a fresh stamp, and an erratum appended to the elder that moves no figure and points forward. The three depths of removal names molt as the gentlest, and this needed no deeper cut.
obs The card's open question is answered in two numbers moving opposite ways: a ReleaseSafe rye is 3.26x smaller and takes 19.5x longer to compile. I recommend neither; what the fleet's time is worth is Keaton's word rather than a measurement's.
obs The pen reached lib/std through a symlink. The walk read the same 552 files at the same bytes, so nothing here depends on the path's characters -- yet it is named on the card as an open question rather than assumed away.
